    "result": "I implemented `RateLimiter` in `solution.py` to the spec, and the 16 tests in `tests/test_solution.py` pass with `python -m pytest -q tests`.\n\nIt keeps a per-client queue of accepted requests and drops entries once `now - t >= window`, so a request exactly one window old no longer counts. `allow` records a request only when it is accepted. `remaining` and `retry_after` record nothing. `retry_after` walks the client's active requests from oldest to newest until enough units would be freed, then returns the time until that request expires.\n\nInvalid `limit`, `window_seconds` or `cost` (including bools) raise `ValueError`. So does any call whose `now` is earlier than the latest `now` the limiter has seen, across all clients.\n\nThe tests cover constructor and cost validation, expiry at exactly one window, rejected requests using no budget, independent clients, costs above 1 with `retry_after`, and the time-went-backwards error on all three methods.\n\nOne choice the spec doesn't settle: a call with an invalid `cost` raises before the time check, so it doesn't advance the latest-time marker.",
